  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1132 AM MDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nWARM AND DRY CONDITIONS:\n\nHigh pressure that has been centered over the central US is getting \nnudged back west by shortwave activity across the northern tier. \nThis will result in dry westerly flow for the next few days. It\nwill also cause temperatures to range from 5-10 degrees above \nnormal. \n\nSome deeper mixing will bring some gusty winds to the surface today \nand tomorrow while humidities hover around 10 to 15%. However, the \ngusty winds will be marginal at best. So despite these values being \nclose to critical fire weather thresholds, only localized critical \nfire weather conditions are expected mainly for the Four Corners \nregion. \n\n\nPOTENTIAL MOISTURE INCREASE:\n\nAs far as our next moisture push, models continue to highlight PWATs \nreaching upwards of 150% of normal for the Friday afternoon onwards. \nThis remains dependent on the evolution and track of an area of low \npressure/trough in the PacNW and the resultant moisture advection \nthanks to a more southerly wind flow. As of now there is fair model \nagreement that this system stays to our north, but it may drive a \nfront into the region. Attm that front seems weak and diffuse by the \ntime it reaches us. If it manages to interact with the moisture in \nplace, we could see some showers and storms. Time will tell, \nhowever, so keep an eye on the latest forecast if you have any big \nweekend plans.",
